# CORRECT, and the landing refused it (its skeptic, probe `a2e`,
# 2026-09-25). `cert` is given back, which empties the cell, and the cell is
# then reused for a getter that hands a reference OUT into it: C writes the
# cell and never reads what it held. The crossing check asked every `@` cell
# not marked `acquires` or `borrows`, so it read the dead value left in the
# cell and aborted before a call that was never going to read it.
#
# An `@` cell marked `retains` is a cell a reference comes out through, like
# `acquires`: the emitter tells the set `hero_handle_retained` on the cell
# after the call, and a C function that READS a handle and adds a reference
# is spelled `retains` on a parameter without `@`, where it is asked.
# `emit/handle_before.hero`'s `reads_cell` holds the rule. The give-back
# sits under an `if` on a value C returns, so the checker's half (panel
# 177's item 3) lets the program reach the runtime.

extern "dead-handle-reused-as-a-retains-out-cell.h"
    record X509 tag ob
    function ob_new() -> X509 acquires ob_put
    function ob_get_shared(@out: X509 retains ob_put) -> i32 when 1
    function ob_put(a: X509 consumes)
    function ob_refs(a: X509) -> i64
    function one() -> i64

function main()
    cert: X509 @= ob_new()

    if one() == 1
        ob_put(a: cert)
    assert ob_get_shared(out: @cert) == 1
    print("references held: ", ob_refs(a: cert))
    ob_put(a: cert)
